To solve this, we first note that triangle \( AHI \) is equilateral, and the lines \(\overline{BC}\), \(\overline{DE}\), and \(\overline{FG}\) are parallel to \(\overline{HI}\). This implies that we have smaller equilateral triangles and trapezoids within the larger equilateral triangle. By dividing the equilateral triangle into smaller parts, we can determine the areas of the relevant regions. Since \( AB = BD = DF = FH \), the triangle is divided into smaller triangles of equal height and width. The area of trapezoid \( FGHI \) is the area of the large triangle minus the area of the four smaller triangles that are cut off by \(\overline{FG}\) parallel to \(\overline{HI}\).
